In article <GBuotBAOB1$zEw9r@crowfeld.demon.co.uk>, Postmaster <crowfeld@crowfeld.demon.co.uk> writes:

>I recently acquired these two boxes along with some VAX Station units.
>I am not very well informed on DEC matters and I wondered if anyone here
>could let me know what these items could be used for?
>
>The DEC Station is model PM32A-KE, has a CD-ROM drive on the front and
>two SCSI disks inside, this one has four 8-meg SIMMs inside (would these
>be standard types?).  There are SCSI and Ethernet ports on the back
>along with connections to a monitor from the graphics adaptor.

I don't know the precise translation of part numbers to front
panel model numbers, but I think the subject offers a good
clue to go with the description.

The DECstation 5000/1xx family use MIPS R3000 CPUs when the 'xx'
is one of 20, 25 and 33.  These two digits indicate the clock
speed.  A later CPU daughter card used a MIPS R4000 at (I think)
50 Mhz.  The memory isn't standard parts.  The built-in SCSI
adapter is slow, narrow, single-ended.  It also pre-dates the
SCSI-2 standard by a couple of years.

>The DEC System is model PM39B-AY, this system has no disks, a plug in
>microcomputer sub-assembly.  There are three memory modules that appear
>to be about 32MB each.  There is a second SCSI interface adaptor fitted,
>along with Ethernet and serial connectors on the back panel.

This would be the DECsystem 5000/240 (not 230).  It uses a 40 Mhz
R3000A.  The DECsystem version of a system simply removed the
graphics subsystem and used a simple tty for the console.  Such
systems were typically used as servers.  The built-in SCSI adapter
and add-on SCSI adapter are basically the same, slow, narrow,
single-ended.  The advantage of the built-in adapter is that is
supports DMA, where the add-on uses a shared chunk of memory
for the host to copy data to/from the adapter.

If the last memory module of three is at the other end of the
slots, it is Prestoserve NVRAM board.  Prestoserve is a combination
NVRAM memory module and software to manage it.  It acts as a non-
volatile write cache for disk writes.  It especially useful for
NFS V2, but also helps with any synchronous writes.

ULTRIX was the operating system at the time the systems were
sold, but one of the free UNIX systems may be ported.  A new
ULTRIX license will cost more than the systems are worth.

