For several  years Intels competitors (PowerPC, DEC) have been making
superior processers (faster, and/or smaller chip area and/or less power
consumption).  This advantage simply has not been large enough to get any
significant fraction of the market to change platforms.  Would I trade in
my pentium (spend the money use a new os, etc) for a machine 1.5 - 2X
faster, if I can't get new software in all cases?  

In order for Intel to take advantage of RISC they will have to make a
machine optimized first for the new instruction set, second for the older
set.   

Intel has 2 things to pull this trick off:

1. Brand Equity (reputation): any they say becomes a self fullfilling
prophecy.  This reputation is not only with the users, but with corporate
purchasers and software developers.  Even before launch they can probably
get the big packages converted and ready to go (MS Office, etc.).

2. Money: Intel has a huge amount of cash.  They can throw several hundred
million dollars at the software industry if needed to aid conversion.  Or more.

The scenario might go like this.  After years of RISC makers say (rightly)
that their systems are better, Intel comes out with a NEW!
INTEL-RISC (tm) technology.  They flood the TV and print media with adds,
and get many major packages to convert.  Suddenly, majically, the public
accepts RISC all at once.  Intel's competitive disadvantage (cisc) has been
eliminated.

The questions are . . .

1. how well will the new chip run X86?
2. how fast will it be in RISC mode?


just an opinion from someone that should know better . . .

