PDP-11 Trainee Guru <geremin@decus.org.au> writes:

> I am not even sure what SMS stands for or if there are/were Australian
> Agents, as part of the system originated in the UK.

SMS were Scientific Micro Systems of Mountain View, California but
they went bankrupt some years ago.  The formatter board is a rather
large item that lives between the Q-Bus interface card and the
floppies and hard disk and contains the logic for the particular
drives in use.

Owning one of these myself (but for a 14" Shugart drive, sorry) I
have kept ads I've seen for service and parts - the only one left
that still seems to be active (this is a holiday weekend in the
USA so it will be next Tuseday before a real person is there to
ask) is Computer Keyways, +1.937.299.8200 - they are in Ohio
which I believe is the Eastern US time zone.

There also used to be a place named Contour Computer Services in
the Silicon Valley area of California that had some former SMS
service staff working there, but they seem to have disappeared.
Perhaps someone knows what happened to them?

I have some documentation but it's only programming and config
data.  SMS also made a special version of XXDP for all this, if
you can find a copy of that it may be some help.

If you are using their double-sided floppy formats and have
trouble reading RX03 floppies on other systems the most likely
cause is track-by-track alternating between sides as opposed
to reading all of one side then all of the other.  Chester
Wilson's DY handler can accomodate this on the controllers
it supports.

If you're using one of their IBM based formats you may want
to start copying things to a DEC format as soon as possible,
I've yet to find anything else that can do this on a Q-Bus
system.

Billy Y..



