Mark E. Levy wrote:
> Unless you can provide the reasons for these crashes, this does not
> refute my point.  The only examples I can find to support your point
> were on improperly configured systems or those with hardware problems.


Simple: crashes requiring a reboot are the result of an application not 
		 adhering to the operating system standards and doing stuff behind 
		 the scenes that isn't quite kosher. And when you have more than one
		 application that isn't structured according to the rules of that
	     operating system, the odds are higher that you'll get a crash.

You can get a DOS system to run forever if the one application that runs
on it
is simple, was written to adhere to whatever basic standards MS made for
DOS 
and never used more efficient ways to bypass the DOS routines.

On VMS however, you tend to have more complex applications with server
processes,
risks of going into RWAST state (and there are some more obscure ones
too), inter process communications that could cause something to hang if
all is not fine and dandy with the messages sent between processes. But
again, it the apps that
really dictate system stability, not the operating system.

Now that most apps that make their way to VMS are really port of the
UNIX or even NT originals, I must ask:
	Which do you think is most stable:
		The version developped for a operating system ?
or		The version develeopped for another op/sys and then ported to your
op/sys?



