Mark E. Levy wrote:
>Bryan Jensen wrote:
>> When a new employee arrives here, the mean time to AXP/VMS workstation
>> crash after first login is about 2 hours.  This time slowly increases
>> over the weeks as the person learns how not to crash the system.
>>
>> Non workstations (and conservatively used workstations) do not crash.
>>
>> While your claim of really long uptimes is correct, one should not
>> take this to mean that all correctly used VMS machines are reliable.
>
>Unless you can provide the reasons for these crashes, this does not
>refute my point.  The only examples I can find to support your point
>were on improperly configured systems or those with hardware problems.

I see crashes from several problems, occuring on several workstations,
booting off two different system disks.  I believe substantial effort
would be required to fix the crashes.

Some of our crashes are likely experienced by other sites, for instance,
our CDE screen saver crashes probably occur at other sites where users
have switched from MS Windows to VMS and think they need those annoying
moving screen savers while the workstation is paused.

Our more serious problems occur in locally written software which displays
3d surfaces with object motion.  These applications are based on PHIGS or 
OpenGL. There may well be nobody else running such applications under VMS.
Judging by the lack of VMS support for Digital's PowerStorm graphics
cards, there must not be a market for MVS graphics.  So it wouldn't
surprise me if we're the only ones with these problems.

Reliability of any operating system will vary greatly depending on
how it's used.  That will make it hard to get consensus on VMS being
more reliable.  Certainly quoting specific uptimes won't convince people.
You can find big values in both the VMS and UNIX world.

From looking at VMS source listing, I believe DEC managers must have
enforced programming standards on the VMS programmers.  There were clearly
no such standards enforced in the writing of UNIX.  One would expect VMS
to be more reliable as a result, and I think this was the case.

Then X windows came along.  The only programming standard governing that
project was that every file had to include the Digital copyright notice.
The same code went into UNIX and VMS.  X window reliability depends on the
support provided after it was written.  After it's written is not a good
time to build in reliability.  I haven't seen any evidence that X is more
reliable under VMS then any UNIX.



