In article <19970831075601.DAA27394@ladder02.news.aol.com>,
Sigstroker <sigstroker@aol.com> wrote:

>Not to mention, where are you going to get enterpise-grade applications
>that run on freeware OS's? Where will you hire experienced system admin's
>that will want to work on a freeware OS? Support from network equipment
>vendors? Etc, etc, etc.
>
>It's clear that anyone that advocates the cheapware approach hasn't spent
>much time in a business environment.

I've spent the past five years working in computing centres in a
business environment, so I do know what's going on there. However,
it's clear just by your use of the word `cheapware' that you don't
understand the use of `free' software in a business environment.

Freely available software software is not free to use in a business
environment. Any software you use has to be supported in some way,
and this support always comes at a price.  That price may be in
the form of paying a lot of money for the software, paying the
software vendor for a support contract, paying a third party for
a support contract, or paying in-house staff to do that support.
Obviously, only the latter two apply to freely available software.

Now, there are a lot of companies that already use the last option
above; in fact, the majority of accounting systems I've seen in
large companies are substantially written in-house and the *only*
support is through in-house support and development staff. How is
doing this with accounting software different from doing it with
operating system software?

It's not hard to get experienced sysadmins to work on a free OS.
In fact, I know a lot of Unix sysadmins personally, and very few
of them have not worked with one of the free OSes at some point.

I'm not sure what you mean by `support from network equipment
vendors.' If you mean `who will write the device driver for this
new network card,' well, you can do it yourself or you can pay
someone to do it. Writing a device driver is no particular feat of
technical skill, and a quick post to a newsgroup or mailing list
associated with the system in question will quickly net you someone
who will be happy to write the driver for a reasonable sum of money.
Finding people to write OS software is no more difficult or expensive
than finding people to write database software.

If instead by `support from network equipment vendors' you mean
`who's going to help me when two pieces of equipment won't talk
properly, and one of them is this freely available OS,' well, you're
exactly where you'd be with two commerical pieces of equipment
anyway. No vendor that I know of will debug network connectivity
between equipment that is and is not from that vendor unless you're
paying them a hefty sum. If you do pay them, they'll debug it for
you regardless of where the other equipment or software comes from.
Last time I went through this in a multi-vendor system with equipment
from two commerical vendors (debugging CHAP authentication between
a Telebit and a Cisco router) both companies made it pretty clear
that if the bug was going to be fixed, I was going to have to
describe it for them in detail. After a few hours work with a packet
sniffer, I found it and walked the Telebit PPP engineer though the
problem. This would have been no different had the Cisco router
been a NetBSD box instead, except that I would have had access to
lower-level debugging tools on the NetBSD box.

Someone else writes:

: Here's another question (apologies to Terry): Who's responsible for
: supporting new hardware with a freeware O/S?

You or whomever you pay to support the hardware. Who's responsible,
for example, for supporting the 3COM 3C905 Ethernet Card under
Digital Unix? How about the Soundblaster 16?

Let me point out now that some of you are no doubt going to come
up with examples of situations where freely available software is
not the correct solution. That's fine; I'm only claiming out that
it's a better solution under *some* circumstances, not all
circumstances. There are certainly plenty of situations where I
would recommend going with vendor software and support.

But there are situations where you have technical expertise in
house and you need certain things (like very quick security fixes)
that vendors supply far too slowly or cannot supply at all. If
that's the case, freely-available software and roll-your-own support
may be the way to go. Certainly many, many companies have been very
successful with roll-your-own support for in-house software in the
past.

cjs
