Ben Scott <bscott@leonardo.sr.unh.edu> writes:

> On 25 Aug 1997, Ketil Z Malde wrote:

>> Because they have to drag with them the x86 instruction set, 

>   <ahem> "Merced" is Intel's code name for the first in a new line of
>   > microprocessors which are *not* x86 compatible.  This will be a
>   completely new > architecture.

Well, your guess is as good as mine as to what Merced will entail.
Rumours I have heard seems to indicate that it will run x86 as well as
PA-RISC instructions, but whether with emulation in software, firmware
or on-chip is not entirely clear.

> The MMX instructions do make a difference -- not as much as some people
> claim,

Does it? I realize that Intel provides an MMX benchmark to ``prove'' the
faster speed, but apart from that - do you know any real applications
that benefit more from the use of MMX than they would from having the
chip real estate turned into even more cache?  I'm not convinced.

> Meanwhile, the reason Intel has most of what could have
> largely been DEC's market share is somewhat due to marketing.

...and being at the right place at the right time.  When the PC captured
the world market, they gained nearly complete market dominance, which
they have been struggling to keep ever since.  With a large degree of
success, one might add.

> > They can't.  A low end Alpha nowadays is a ~400Mhz 21164, with roughly
> > the same performance as a 266Mhz PII.  And roughly the same cost.

>   Low-end for you, maybe.

Huh - tell that to my old '486.  But that's what is sold as new machines
these days.  I don't think it's fair to compare second hand Multia boxen
to new PCs.

>> Intel is stagnating technically, but they are still excellent marketeers.

>   Intel is not the technical fool you make it out to be.  They've maintained
> original native architecture compatibility for almost 20 years with the x86
> line, while adding a huge increase in capability and speed.  That's
> not easy. 

Course not.  I agree, it's an impressive feat.  But since the Pentium
(which was introduced many years ago), I don't think they have improved
significantly.  And to wit, the Pentium still outsells any other chip
they make in PC systems, even two generations later.

And while the Pentium Pro was a technical advance with OOOE etc, and
thus gained a few percent of equally clocked Pentium, it failed to
achieve significantly higher clock rates one would expect.  And now the
Pentium II is roughly similar in performance to the Pro at the same
clock.

The real change from Pro to Pentium II is the Slot-1 socket, which as we
speak is being replaced with Slot-2 that the next generation Intel chips
will use.  It appears Intel is not happy with just selling us a new
processor every two years, now it wants us to replace our motherboard as
well.

And the K6 seems to be performing at Pentium II speeds in good, old
Socket-7 motherboards, so apparently the reason for introducing a new
socket every once in a while does not have anything to do with
performance. 

> But if you blindly assume Intel can't design a commercial
> microprocessor -- the very thing they invented -- then you're sadly
> mistaken.  DEC needs to watch out.

I feel obliged to point out that I never thought IA64 Merced wouldn't
become a great success.  I just don't think they will be leading in
terms of performance. 

~kzm
