        Jim  Mehlhop   <jmehlhop@cisco.com>   already  commented,  quite
    correctly,   that  your  current  parameters  for  VOTES   vis-a-vis
    EXPECTED_VOTES could result in a fragmented cluster.  Not good.   As
    he  said,  _if_ you had at least one disk on a shared bus, i.e., the
    CI or DSSI (can DSSI do  three  hosts?), then you could use a quorum
    disk.   This would be the "best" solution, but I doubt you have  the
    equipment to implement it.

        Let me observe, however, that  you're  taking down the two VAXen
    nightly  anyway, so why do you need them to contribute votes to  the
    cluster?  Are you really in a position to have, or want, the cluster
    to continue if the Alpha goes down but the VAXen stay up?   That  is
    the only reason to have them contribute votes.

        I'd suggest instead that you  set  VOTES=1 on the Alpha, VOTES=0
    on  the  two VAXen, and EXPECTED_VOTES=1 on all nodes.   With  these
    settings, the VAXen can come and go as they please.   It  does  mean
    that  the  VAXen can't form a cluster until the Alpha is booted, but
    in your situation that may not be an issue...

        -Ken F.
