Glenn Everhart wrote:

> Conclusion: If you're sending mail to someone inside DEC (except
> for the moment VMS engineering, still using VMS mail MOSTLY), better
> use US mail. Or telephone, or something that you trust not to lose
> the message, either for days or permanently. VMS mail, gated to
> SMTP on the net, is on the other hand pretty robust in that messages
> get there or get sent, and don't routinely suffer delays of hours
> or days, and as long as no RMS bugs bite you (the last was, what,
> a couple years ago, and a patch appeared) you don't lose things.

Oh how true. Up until less than a year ago I would regularly email
logfiles and the like to staff at the UK CSC. Turnaround was swift and
nothing ever got lost. Nowadays I'm usually asked not to as there are
enormous delays since they switched to Exchange and (I was told) they've
suffered from several complete and utter losses of all queued mail. And
this is what customers are expected to switch to?

Sad to say but perhaps it's time VMS engineering was sold off.
Preferably to VMS staff themselves. It's amazing that I see more
publicity for VMS products such as Multinet, Purveyor, PMDF etc. via
mailshots and phone calls than I do for DEC layered products.

We keep seeing statements here about once a year from Digital saying
that Digital is committed to the continued develpment and support of VMS
and then we see another half-dozen layered products sold off/dropped.
Someone at Digital needs to see a shrink as they're obviously suffering
from a rather nasty case of schizophrenia I'm afraid.

