In article <340471f7.1568064@nntp.netcomuk.co.uk>,
rstevens@netcomuk.co.uk (Rob) wrote:
> I have an Alphaserver 2100 and 2 Vax 4000's running VMS 6.2. The 2100
> now has to remain up all the time, through the night, whilst the Vaxes
> have to be shutdown/removed from the cluster every night. I won't bore
> you with the details, but doing this is for security reasons. (The
> 2100 being in a vault and the Vaxes outside the vault)
> 
> Currently, all three members have VOTES=1, EXPECTED_VOTES=1, which was
> fine for a full cluster shutdown like we used to do. However, now I'm
> removing the two Vaxes in the evening, the 2100 loses quorum when both
> have left. I looked at the CL_VOTES, CL_EXPECTED and CL_QUORUM with
> show/clus and found quorum to be 2, which accounts for the problem. 
> 
> The trouble is, I thought, according to my calculations, that quorum
> should be 1 in that case..

No, quorum should be 2 in your case.  (3 votes +1)/2

BTW, setting EXPECTED_VOTES=1 when obviously the expected votes being
3 is a bad idea.  You can get multiple single node clusters and corruption
of any disks.  (if the cluster forms correctly VMS sees EXPECTED_VOTES being
bogus on the fly and adjusts things accordingly)

> So, how do I remove two of the three machines without stopping the
> other. I'm aware that I've probably made a hash up of my calculations,
> and would appreciate your thoughts/ideas...

Do you want the 4000s to keep running if the Alpha happens to crash? Or is
the Alpha essential to whatever you are doing?

If you don't expect/want/need the VAXes to stay up, the simplest solution
is to set VOTES=1, EXPECTED_VOTES=1 on the Alpha, and VOTES=0, EXPECTED_VOTES=1
on the VAXes.

-Mike



