Curt Sampson wrote:
> 
> In article <3400BC1D.6F21@world.std.com>,
> Terry Shannon  <shannon@world.std.com> wrote:
> 
> >The
> >Good Thing about Linux (other, of course, than the fact that it's free
> >and you can modify the kernel, etc) is that it provides an opportunity
> >for DEC and others to sell their Unixes as back-end servers to Linux.
> 
> What exactly do you mean by this?

Exactly what I said. Folks within DEC's Unix camp (maddog Hall,
f'rinstance) promote Linux as a driver of Alpha, and hopefully DUNIX,
sales. The rationale seems to be that in extremely cost-sensitive
markets (education), Linux makes good sense on the desktop. I can't
argue with that logic. DEC also believes that DUNIX makes an excellent
back-end server to Linux clients.

> 
> I think it says something that NASA Ames Research Labs recently
> set up an Alphaserver 8400 with 2 GB of RAM and 3.5 TB of disk
> storage, and is running NetBSD instead of Digital Unix on it. In
> three or four years the support for multiprocessing and other
> `high-end' features on these free operating systems is going to be
> right up there with where the major vendors are now.
> 
> What effect this is going to have on the market I don't think I'll
> try to predict. :-)

Applications availability and service/support are likely to be gating
factors. Still, I'd be more than a little concerned about the freeware
Unix phenomenon if I happened to be a Unix-centric vendor.

Incidentally, how did NASA manage to scarf up a TurboLaser without an
OS? Or, did they purchase a Digital UNIX-based TLaser, then trash DUNIX
in favor of NetBSD?

Terry Shannon





