Well, Digital seems to be getting out of those parts that have become
commodity. (disks, networking, printers, low end PCs, low end
workstations etc).

The problem is that what used to be a big networking company (remember
"the network is the computer" ?) is being pushed out of its original
niches by a quickly changing market which is becoming increasingly
commodity driven.


There will be an increasingly very small niche in which DEC will be able
to succeed. With Tandem now a COMPAQ subsidiary, I suspect some of the
niches DEC hopes to keep will also becoem commodity.


So, my peave is that instead of tackling the commodity phenomena in
which some companies are able to succeed, Digital is retreating and
hiding in obscure places where it hopes to remain unchallenged. Fix your
problems Digital instead of hiding them under a thick rug because soon
enough, the cleaners will come and take that rug away and you won't have
anywhere left to hide.



