I am pleased to report that Shannon Knows DEC is Y2K-ready. Contingency
plans are in place to rename the publication Shannon Knows Tech. But I
don't think it will come to that.

Incidentally, here's my take on the demise of Digital Age. It's the
commentary from the next issue of SKD...


RIP Digital Age
(c) 1997 by Terry C. Shannon

Digital Age, the sole remaining Digital-specific trade publication, has
shuffled off its mortal coils. The August 1997 issue of the magazine
serves as an obituary for a once-vibrant marketplace populated by half a
dozen Digital-specific trade publications here in the US, and several
additional titles in the United Kingdom, Europe, and Australia.


Ironically, Digital Age, in its initial incarnation as DEC Professional,
was one of two pioneering DEC market magazines published by Dave Mallery
and Carl Marbach's Professional Press. Carl and Dave created the
prototype DEC magazine, the subscription-based RSTS Professional, back
in the late 1970s. First distributed via a guerilla marketing campaign
at a DECUS symposium, RSTS Pro soon became a profitable venture.
Accordingly, DEC Professional was launched in 1982 as a
controlled-circulation (advertiser-subsidized) magazine that covered the
entire Digital and DEC-compatible hardware and software marketplace. On
a personal note, my first published article appeared in the May 1983
issue of DEC Professional.

Sadly, this is the third obituary I've written for a DEC magazine in the
last year and a half. (Longtime SKD readers will recall the Spring 1996
demise of DEC User, and the Fall 1996 passing of Digital News&Review.)
Does Strike Three mean that Digital is out?


Not necessarily. While one might argue that DEC has withered to the
point that it can no longer sustain even a single controlled-circulation
vendor-specific publication, and while it's evident that the firm has
lost its last free-of-charge independent forum, the fact remains that
the marketplace has changed. Indeed, the vast majority of the software
and DEC-compatible hardware vendors whose advertisements sponsored DEC
Professional, Digital Review, Hardcopy, et al, no longer exist or have
reinvented themselves and focused on new markets. 


The increasing commoditization of the hardware market, and the rise of
WNT and Unix shrinkwrapped applications, has marginalized many erstwhile
businesses. The decline of technical sessions at DECUS symposia is
mirrored by declining interest in VMS internals and related
"propellerhead" issues that once provided grist for the trade press
mill. Consolidation, merger, and acquisition in the software business
has reduced the number of ISVs who cater to the DEC market. The decline
of platform-specificity in the open systems era undoubtedly is the last
nail in the controlled-circulation publication coffin.


The absence of an advertiser-subsidized information vehicle for the DEC
marketplace creates a problem for vendors who now lack a forum for press
releases, and users who now lack an independent source of information
and product  reviews. While Shannon Knows DEC is by no means free of
charge, I will strive to provide more coverage of DEC-market vendors and
their products, and more hands-on product review as well. 


As one of the sole remaining sources of information on things Digital,
I�ve pretty much inherited the coverage I once relegated to the trade
press. As always, I appreciate reader feedback, and I'd appreciate
hearing your thoughts and suggestions on SKD's editorial content. Feel
free to drop me an email or give me a call. I'll be looking forward to
it!

Terry Shannon
Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC



