X-Deja-AN: 268051651
References: <33F49A99.2A21@swcp.com>
Organization: ducky.net / Portland, OR
Newsgroups: comp.arch,comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec


In article <33F49A99.2A21@swcp.com>, H.W. Stockman wrote:
>The old Stream benchmark page (which seems
>currently unavailable) listed about a factor
>1.5 or so improvement for the Intel Alder
>PPro system, relative to real, production PPro
>systems with EDO RAM.  I'd swear the Stream page
>stated that the Alder system had SDRAM, but that
>doesn't seem possible; www.specbench.org lists the
>Alder with 60ns FPM DRAM.  The 440LX chipset is
>just around the corner, but 100 MHz bus speed
>is apparently not.

The Alder was based on the Orion chipset, which doesn't support
any fancy memory types, not even EDO.  However, it *does*
support 4-way interleaved memory.  Memory accesses are
simultaneously sent to all four interleaved banks, and the
returned values are sequentially burst back to the CPU.

This means that memory timings on a fully populated Alder's
66 MHz bus are something like N-1-1-1 cycles per 32-byte burst
transfer, in contrast to the more typical N-2-2-2 bursts seen
in 66 MHz EDO systems.  I don't remember what N is for the
Alder but I think it's about 8.

The problem with interleaving is you need lots of SIMMs and
lots of wires.  It's not a mass-market technology.

But, right now if you can do a sufficient amount of interleaving,
EDO and SDRAM offer no bandwidth improvements over plain FPM.
They may offer latency improvements in the future, especially
as they become more mainstream and hence the focus of future
DRAM improvements.



