In article <33FE3D12.1388@world.std.com>, Terry Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>> Question for you. Summarise the benefits to Digital of being in the NT party in 200 words.
>> 
>> Go on I dare you.
>
>$1.6B in revenue in FY97.

I wonder what they are counting in that total.  How much of that is NT on
Alpha?  Besides, where do they announce the bookkeeping on the sales of 
their own products that they forfeited to obtain this number?  1 million
Exchange seats is how much of a loss per seat for Digital versus
maintaining their All-in-1 customers? 

Anyway, for the sake of argument, if we guess that $ 1 Billion of this is
WNT on Alpha sales it indicates quite clearly their immense risk exposure.
No other hardware manufacturer has allowed Microsoft this sort of
inevitably fatal stranglehold.  Companies building WNT/Intel machines are
safe because Microsoft cannot easily change the OS to selectively kill one
specific manufacturer - it either works on all Intel machines or on none of
them. Digital, on the other hand, by demanding WNT on Alpha, put the noose
around their own necks, climbed up on the platform, stood over the trap
door, and told Bill Gates how to work the release lever.  The only reason
that I can fathom that he hasn't pulled the lever yet is that he has not
finished making Digital force 100% of their customers into Microsoft's
hands, or turn over the last shreds of their key technologies.  I mean, if
he killed Digital now, some of its customers would be free to choose
nonMicrosoft solutions - can't have that.  Digital still maintains a
corporate pipe dream that WNT sales on Alpha will explode so that Digital
will become so important to Microsoft that the latter will not want to snap
their silly little necks.  It just isn't going to happen - the desktop
Intel PCs are now so fast that only power users can find a way to soak up
all those cycles, so Alpha's speed advantage is devalued practically to
zero.  (For instance, the latest Consumer Reports suggested that buyers
weight convenience and other features, since every machine they looked at
was more than fast enough.) Even on servers the CPU is rarely the limiting
factor. 

>Uh, margins are up.
>
>One area in which Digital did do better in FY97 is margins. Indeed,
>during 4FQ97, Digital recorded its best gross margin in more than three
>years. Gross margin improved to 34.6 percent from 33.4 percent in the
>third quarter and improved a full two points from the 32.6 percent
>posted in 4FQ96. Product gross margin was 37.2 percent compared with
>35.3 percent in 3FQ97 and showed a three point improvement over the 34.3
>percent in 4FQ97. 

That is more or less what you'd expect if their low and midrange sales were 
fading, since high end sales have the largest margins.  (In the limit they 
end up with tiny market with huge margins.)  What you want to see is margin 
improvements on the same product mix - meaning that they are building 
things more efficiently.  That isn't a conclusion we can make from the data 
presented, but we can't exclude it either.

Margin numbers for service can be similarly biased - there is much more 
competition for service on PCs and small servers than there is on larger 
machines.  (When was the last time you saw a turbolaser hauled into 
ComputerCity for work?)

