In article <33F48F7C.52A@gecm.com>, Dave Miller <dave.m.miller@gecm.com> writes:
> We're about to upgrade a mixed VMS 6.1 cluster to VMS 6.2. Are there any
> gothcas we should be aware of? We're currently running DEC Fortran 6.2
> and DEC Pascal 5.2. Is there mileage in upgrading these to 7.1 and 5.5
> respectively?

The main 6.2 specific "gotcha" is a VAX QMAN bug which will cause
SYS$COMMON:[SYSEXE]SYS$QUEUE_MANAGER.QMAN$JOURNAL to grow to 41k blocks after
the reboot when you've done the upgrade. Download VAXQMAN03_070 from
www.service.digital.com 's public access area (the _070 is correct because the
patch is for OpenVMS VAX V5.5-2, V5.5-2H4 V5.5-2HF, V6.0, V6.1, V6.2 and V7.0).
You might also want to get VAXACRT09_062 which fixes a few C run time library
bugs, and you'll _definitely_ want to get and apply VAXLIBR06_070 which fixes
the 9999 day "delta time" limit that can cause tight CPU loop problems with
certain types of application (notably ones that use threads). VMS 6.2 is a very
stable and reliable release IMHO.

 If you have access to Digital Service Network Interactive Text Search you can
enter queries like "Which ECOs need to be applied to VMS 6.2" for a
comprehensive list. By the way, does anyone know if there's internet access to
the DSN ITS database ? 

         - Phil Ottewell

