fairfield@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu wrote:
> 
>         Running VMS 6.2/6.2-1H3, we have some _legacy_ EXABYTE 8200's in
>     MTI MA24 controllers attached to an HSC70.
> 
>         The 8200's  are  getting  very  hard  to  keep operational.  Our
>     maintenance  organization  wants to replace the 8200's with  8500's,
>     plus upgrade the firmware in the MA24 to correspond.
> 
>         Problem: How to we set the 8500's to _write_ at _low_ density so
>     the tapes thus written can still be read on an 8200?
> 
>         I seem to recall postings that said there was a switch or jumper
>     "on the back"  of  the  8500,  but  all  I  see  are the unit-select
>     dip-switches,  and  a  number of jumper-less  jumper  posts.   These
>     8500's are _not_ in a table-top enclosure, but  are  mounted  "bare"
>     within  the MA24.  I also seem to recall posts saying that VMS mount
>     commands would _not_ switch an 8500 to low density for write.
> 
>         Any comments would be gladly received.
> 
>             Thanks, Ken
> --
>  Kenneth H. Fairfield       |  Internet: Fairfield@Slac.Stanford.Edu
>  SLAC, P.O.Box 4349, MS 46  |  DECnet:   45537::FAIRFIELD (45537=SLACVX)
>  Stanford, CA   94309       |  Voice:    650-926-2924    FAX: 650-926-3515
>  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
>  These opinions are mine, not SLAC's, Stanford's, nor the DOE's...

The answer is in the old "/DENSITY=" qualifier.   When initing the tape
and mounting the tape (or with the BACKUP command), use the qualifier
"/DENSITY=1600" to force the tape driver to use the 2.5GB density and
use "/DENSITY=6250" to force the tape drive to use the 5.0GB density.


