Yes, if you look at a specific point in time such as today, this may be
true. 
But looking at both the past and future, Digital has to decide how it is
to fix its mistakes of the past (adverts telling folks to migrate from
VMS to UNIX, calling VMS "legacy" etc). So, even if providing low cost
VMS costs DEC a bit of money, it may still be a cheaper solution to fix
VMS's image problem which was self-inflicted by DEC.

Also, in these days where sheep are driven by hype and media, showing
momentum of VMS with a huge increase in the number of licenses sold
(even if DEC doesn't make much money on them) would go a long way to
kill the rumour of VMS's impending death.

>         - other software licensing prices (and those product managers
>                 have to determine how much they can affort to charge)

Doesn't take a lot of time to figure it out: Take the price of the
equivalent Microsoft product, and add %10 and that is your price. MS
can't complain you are competeting against them, and 10% is perhaps an
acceptable markup if you are getting better quality. 

In a captive environment, it is true that Digital would loose megabucks
if it provided a way for users to get compilers at MS prices instead of
having to buy the compiler for their huge mainframe cluster. I guess
this mentality was perhaps semi-valid in the 80s.

But these days, if your layered products are too expensive, folks simply
move to another platform and you loose them as customers. period. DEC
has no choice but to be competitive against MS.

>         - more resources will need to be available for support, and
>                 there would be competition with the same support
>                 resources for people buying the higher-margin systems

As long as increased support requirements are the result of increased
sales, then it is not a problem to hire more folks. It is called GROWTH.
I know this is foreign to Digital these days, but other companies have
experienced it and are still alive :-)

As far as priority calling, just look at how companies such as ADOBE
provide their support. Those who need fast response dial a separate
number and get through much faster.


>         - the supply chain has to understand the market and sell it
>                 correctly - or lose integrity

This is something Digital has not been able to do anyways. So providing
low cost VMS won't change that. But it will at least allow DEC's
remaining loyal customers to keep VMS in their shops and perhaps
convince a few more to adopt VMS. If the word gets around, the good old
"word of mouth" may take off and once the media gets a hold of this,
then the media hype can do its magic and propell Digital past Microsoft
and IBM (ok,ok, this is dreaming...)

The point: Yes, doing it right would cost megabucks to Digital since it
would have to learn how to do marketing, get a decent commodity sales
channel etc. 
Nut we all now Digital is incapable of doing so. But at least, if
Digital allowed us to do the sales pitch by providing a competitively
priced VMS, it would be better than allowing VMS to wither as a
"mainframe only" system.



> On the other hand, low-end sales, with their lower margins (or even
> small losses) may still be worth if - *if* those sales translated into
> greater sales in the higer-margin systems.

Yeah, but look at the cost of NOT doing so. It make cause more rapid
erosion of larger systems.



