This idea sounds good on the surface, but it seems to make an
assumption that it will cost Digital $0 to do this selling.  This
tends not to be the case considering other parts of the business, such
as:
	- marketing resources (compared to other places to put thos
		resources - remember "opportunity cost" from 
		economics 101?)
	- other software licensing prices (and those product managers
		have to determine how much they can affort to charge) 
	- more resources will need to be available for support, and
		there would be competition with the same support
		resources for people buying the higher-margin systems
	- the supply chain has to understand the market and sell it 
		correctly - or lose integrity


Putting a product into a market really requires a significant
commitment of business resources.  At best you may be working on some
slim margin, and the worst case is that you actually end up losing
money when the entire business is taken into account.  In the end, it
could actually damage Digital's credibility even further (although
sometimes I can't imagine how) if they are not fully prepared for this
business.

On the other hand, low-end sales, with their lower margins (or even
small losses) may still be worth if - *if* those sales translated into
greater sales in the higer-margin systems.

>
>By making VMS affordable, you will bring additional applications, users
>and make it a more interesting platform and this can only help large
>customers who will have an easier time convincing their suppliers to
>make their apps run on VMS.

I agree completely here.  Much of the earlier success of computing
platforms seemed to be boosted by adoption of low-cost systems for
education and garage-based developers.  For examples, various Apple
computers, earlier VAX systems (colleges and universities used to have
lots of these), and Unix systems.  I think a lot of the success of
these systems could be traced back to their familiarity from the
education system.  And some enjoyed additional market boost by making
it relatively cheap for developers to obtain and use.
