>  The VAX-11/750 has sockets for four boot ROMs, labeled "A" thru "D"
>  on the front panel selector switch.  "A" is usually the DDA0: device,
>  the console TU58 drive.  "D" is usually the local system's primary
>  disk drive.

You know what?  My 11/750 HAD "A" to "D" boot option selections, but starting
with VMS 4.0 (I think) the "D" option was removed to make room for additional
microcode instructions (this according to the tech who did the work).  The
11/750 then required a TU58 with microcode on it to boot correctly.  Before
that, I didn't need to use the console at all, after that change, it was
essential.

