In article <33f73524.0@oit.umass.edu>, rjb@lessing.oit.umass.edu (Richard J Bishop) writes:

:I recently acquired an old VaxStation 3200. After rewiring the inside (a lot 
:of the connectors were disconnected), I got it to start OK. However, I'm 
:having a problem doing anything with it. This is what I've got so far:
..
:With selector to Run, rear display shows 'A'. Serial port displays
..
:Prompt accepts some commands. An attempt to boot with 'b', 'b /1' 'b /2' etc 
:scrolls a few lines and then give ?84 and the >>> prompt. When this happens, 
:back display changes to a 4, and then a 9.
:
:How do I get this machine to boot. Is there a problem with the disk, or does 
:the disk not have any operating system on it?

  You have one or more hardware problems uncovered by the self-test.
  (This system is not near booting from the disk...)

  A LED code of `A' -- if memory serves -- is the graphics controller.
  Remove all controllers and modules other than the CPU and the memory,
  and see how far the self-test gets.

  I will assume you are familiar with the BA23/BA123/BA2xx series Q22-bus
  enclosure, and with the serpentine Q-bus wiring on the BA23 and BA123,
  and with things like grant cards and CSRs.  (If you are not, you'll need
  to become familiar with these concepts before proceeding -- without the
  correct Q-bus configuration and the correct CSRs and Interrupt Vector
  settings, the system will normally not operate as expected.)  I will
  also assume you are familiar with working with static-sensitive devices.

  The KA650 CPU System Maintenance Manual (EK-180AA-MG) includes tables
  with the self-test descriptive information, and most the likely cause
  of the failure(s), and the field-replaceable-units (FTUs).

