In article <Pine.PMDF.3.91.970818013758.826440341A-100000@alpha2.curtin.edu.au>, "Paul Repacholi (prep)" <zrepachol@alpha2.curtin.edu.au> writes:
:Yes, that IS 750!
:Can someone fill in the required majic to try fireing it up
:from an RA-81 on a UDA-50 please.

  Switch the VAX-11/750 BOOT PROM selector to (usually) the "D" PROM,
  wave the rubber chicken over the disk array, press the reset button,
  and (if it doesn't start booting), try "B" or "B DUA0" at the >>>.

  If that does not work, then you will need to sacrifice a console TU58
  cassette by sliding it into the console drive slot, switch the boot
  PROM selector to (usually) "A", press reset, and try "B/800 DDA0".
  (The TU58 drive usually returns all such sacrifices unharmed...)

  The latter command will trigger BOOT58 genie, where you can then
  request the invocation of a boot command procedure such as DEFBOO
  (usually via @DEFBOO.CMD), GENBOO.CMD, or DUABOO.CMD, and perform
  a conversational bootstrap.  If DEFBOO (or others) is not present
  or is correctly configured, you may (will?) need to watch the
  commands displayed by DEFBOO when invoked via BOOT58, and then
  restart the boot process, and then reenter the appropriate commands
  for your local hardware configuration manually.  (See below.)

  BOOT58 bypasses the bootblock-based lookup of VMB, loading and using
  a copy of the VMB.EXE image off the TU58. (If you know you'll need to
  WRITEBOOT the disk, you will have to use BOOT58 to bypass the missing
  bootblock reference to VMB.  See below.)

  And awaken the RA81 via the <RUN> button on the drive, and select both
  the "A" and "B" ports.  (At least until you figure out which port is
  wired to the UDA50 controller...)

  Here are the ancient runes sometimes necessary for casting the
  VAX-11/750 bootstrap spell:

	>>>D/G 0 11                ! the UDA50 device type code
	>>>D/G 1 FFE000            ! Base of UNIBUS I/O page for UB0
	>>>D/G 2 3F468             ! CSR addr offset of UDA50 on UB0
	>>>D/G 3 0                 ! boot from UDA50 controller unit 0
	>>>D/G 4 0                 ! unused on VAX-11/750
	>>>D/G 5 0                 ! Software boot flags (`R5' used by VMB)
	>>>D/G E 200               ! Addr of working memory plus %x200

  If you have an Remote Diagnostic Module (RDM; L0006) module present in
  the CMI, you can avoid awakening the BOOT58 genie, as the RDM has a
  "load" command -- otherwise, you get to use BOOT58.  If you have an
  RDM, press <CTRL/D> to get to the RDM> prompt, then...

	RDM>LO VMB.EXE 200         ! Load primary bootstrap at addr 200
	RDM>RET                    ! back to the plebian >>> prompt
	>>>START 200               ! Start running the code at addr 200

  Otherwise, if you have a corrupt bootblock or a missing DEFBOO.CMD,
  you will have to re-cast the entire spell -- except the RET, of course
  -- at the BOOT58> prompt.  (In order to get the bootstrap loaded, and
  copious amounts of alcohol do not work, one needs BOOT58 or the RDM.)

  Once you have awakened the spirit of OpenVMS in the VAX-11/750, use
  the SYS$UPDATE:CONSCOPY procedure to upload a copy of the console TU58,
  fix DEFBOO.CMD, then CONSCOPY it back.  (Creating a backup TU58 might
  also be useful, in the event of an unintentional cassette sacrifice.)
  And use WRITEBOOT to rewrite the pointer in the bootblock to the current
  VMB LBN -- both now, and after any time the VMB image is moved.

  Other bootstrap configurations, and bootstraps via the CI750, will
  require continued use of BOOT58, and access to additional runes.

  For information on how to acquire access to the system passwords
  from the console, please see the OpenVMS FAQ -- the incantations
  one must specify to R5 and to SYSBOOT> are covered there.

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
