In article <33F84DF9.5937@world.std.com>, Terry Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>C'est vrai. I fully agree with you about volumes and minimal incremental
>support expenses on low-end VMS. I believe DEC feels such a pricing
>decision would harm their ability to charge extremely high prices for
                                             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>workgroup, departmental, and enterprise licenses.

Ah yes, the infamous "greedy and stupid" strategy.

Digital can already start kissing the workgroup and departmental markets
goodbye as reasonably priced WNT servers (on Intel, naturally) replace all
of those OpenVMS VAXes and Alphas, and many DU boxes to boot.  Digital will
be safe on the enterprise level for another couple of years though. They
must feel quite proud to have ceded 99% of the general computing market in
order to really lock up that one really lucrative niche.  But is it really
all that locked up?  Can you even imagine the resources that Microsoft will
have to throw against them, once it controls 100% of the desktops plus low,
middle, and even middle-high range servers?  This may take 5 or 10 years to
take place, but by not putting up a fight at the lower levels, Digital (and
even IBM) are guaranteeing that their high end systems will be running
Microsoft OS's in 5-10 years. Assuming, that is, that Microsoft deigns to
allow their continued existence, and even bothers making WNT "N"
run on these company's hardware.  More likely Microsoft's
strategy will be to champion some other hardware vendor with a less 
entrenched or nonexistant high end OS system, NOT make WNT "N" run on any
competing mainframe class machine, and use the chosen vendor to crush
Digital and IBM at one fell swoop. I think that Microsoft has already
chosen HP for this task, although HP may not really be aware of it at this
time.  At least, I think that's what the real strategy is behind
Microsoft's agreement with HP.  Since Microsoft plans for 5 to 10 years down
the line they would need to be lining up the deliverer of the coup de grace
right about now.  

[Explanation of the scenario described above.  Bill Gates' plan for 
Microsoft is clear, he wants absolute control of all OS's on everything.
So far this is working out pretty much as he planned.  Windows is on just
about every PC, Windows NT is on low and midrange servers, and Microsoft
OS's are finding their way into palmtops, WebTV, and other consumer niches.
That leaves the high end, where he faces an entrenched IBM, and a somewhat
less entrenched, but technologically formidable Digital presence.  The
Microsoft strategy is a long term strategy - they want the high end, but
they know they can wait 5 or 10 years to get there, especially if in the 
meantime they lock up all servers smaller than mainframes.  

The first possible method to achieve mainframe dominance would be to coopt
one of the existing bigger players.  However, IBM has been burned
repeatedly by Microsoft (the original PC deal, OS/2) and they have no
intention of ceding their mainframe goldmine to Microsoft.  Digital is a huge
question mark.  They have cooperated on WNT, and in general, rolled over
and played dead whenever Microsoft asked them to, but it is unclear whether
they can be counted on to cannibalize their own extremely lucrative OpenVMS
and Digital Unix megaservers.  

So a second plan has to to be in motion - Microsoft needs
another player that they can use to crack open this market.  They size up
the potential players and find HP, SGI, and Sun, with companies like Dell
as long shot wildcards. Since Sun is essentially fighting a guerrila war
against Microsoft dominance through Java, and Sun management is actively
Microsoft hostile, it is pretty easy to drop them off the list. That leaves
HP and SGI.  HP is the more attractive of the two.  For one thing, it is
already partially into the Microsoft camp, selling Windows and WindowsNT
workstations.  Secondly, they are in bed with Intel developing the next 
generation chip on which such a system might be based.  Lastly, they
make a lot more than just computers, and might be willing to cede the
software part of their computer sales in exchange for enhancing their
hardware and peripheral sales by a greater amount.  So Microsoft sends over
an advance party, talks are held, everything goes well, and they iron out a
partnership.  Odds are that HP does not even know yet that it is being
groomed for this starring role. 

From the Microsoft perspective, this is pretty much a win/win/win strategy.
If the midrange WNT machines simply grow up and over the mainframes, then 
Microsoft wins. If Digital rolls over and kills its own high end OS's
and embraces WNT, then Microsoft wins - because they can market their
software on Digital's hardware and cut into IBM's market.  Lastly, they can
develop WNT "N" to run on very high end HPs, terminate WNT development on
Alpha for some pretext or another, and so smash Digital financially. They
can then begin the feast by consuming Digital's erstwhile market share, and
still have a tool with which to carve up IBMs segment of the mainframe
market.] 

Regards,

