as a technical editor, then went to Digital Review where I was
reincarnated as Charlie Matco. I've been a columnist for Digital News,
Digital News and Review, DEC User, and a defunct Aussie magazine called
ON$DEC. It's sad to see all these books consigned to Jurassic Park.

It may also be
> the last independent publication which was DEC specific, and contained
> product announcements, reviews, and advertising.  (SKD is a different
> sort of beast, and still exists.)

You bet SKD still exists! Matter of fact, I've done a couple of product
reviews and plan to do more in the near future. I don't take any vendor
advertising, but I try to cover significant announcements from DEC ISVs
and partners, even a little competitive stuff just to make things more
interesting.

> 
> Ignoring, if that's possible, the symbolism of the passing of a publication
> named "Digital Age", there will be some immediate practical repercussions
> in the Digital marketplace.
> 
>  1.  There is no longer any independent publication which can reasonably be
>      expected to review products for Digital systems.  Indeed, I'm not
>      entirely sure where to look now even for new product announcements,
>      other than in InFORM.
> 
>  2.  There is no longer an independent publication where vendors can advertise
>      their products solely to the Digital audience.  This is expecially
>      bad news for the Digital hardware reseller market.  Not only will we
>      not see whatever monthly specials these folks have, but potential
>      buyers will likely have a very hard time even finding out about
>      the existence of these vendors.
> 
>  3.  Other than these newsgroups and DECUS, there will be no other
>      public forum for any sort of public communication between end users
>      and suppliers.  That means less sharing of information.

From time to time, I post material from my newsletter on the Harvard
Research Group website, http://www.hrgresearch.com 

My goal of course is to generate subscriptions to the newsletter, but I
figure it doesn't hurt to disseminate information that's a bit dated but
still interesting. Heck, somebody's gotta do it.
> 
> Although I suppose they will ignore this event, or more likely, never even
> know that it took place, the management and board of Digital should
> seriously consider what it means when their company cannot garner
> sufficient mindshare to support the existence of even a single such
> publication, and is otherwise all but invisible in the rest of the trade
> press.

I have to agree with you. Digital management apparently is more
interested in targeting new customers than the installed base. The
"Inform" advertorial magazine is nothing but rehashed press releases.
Most of the trade publications give Digital very little coverage.

What's left now is:

Shannon Knows DEC: 4-6 pages, 24x/year, $395 USA, $450 elsewhere

Digital Directions Report, Computer Economics, Carlsbad, CA: 6-8 pages
12x/year $495 USA

Digital Systems Journal, Computer Economics, Carlsbad, CA: ~30 pages, 4x
or 6x/year $80 USA

Apologies to Computer Economics if I have pricing wrong; this is off the
top of my head.
 
Terry Shannon
