I'd like to know the impact SDRAM will likely
have on performance of a program that looks
a lot like McCalpin's "Stream"; that is, an
application that accesses memory more or less
sequentially, but has very little data reuse
(for example a local filter, but not a blocked
matrix multiplication).


Processors of interest:

Intel PPro and PII

DEC 21164x

...with main memory clocked at 66 MHz.

The old Stream benchmark page (which seems
currently unavailable) listed about a factor
1.5 or so improvement for the Intel Alder
PPro system, relative to real, production PPro
systems with EDO RAM.  I'd swear the Stream page
stated that the Alder system had SDRAM, but that
doesn't seem possible; www.specbench.org lists the
Alder with 60ns FPM DRAM.  The 440LX chipset is
just around the corner, but 100 MHz bus speed
is apparently not.

If anyone has real data on the effects of
interleaving or other lower-tech memory
improvements, vs. SDRAM, I'd appreciate that info
too.

I can't find much info on 21164x systems with
SDRAM; Digital lists ECC SDRAM as available for
433 MHz 21164 systems, but the cheaper systems
(e.g. Enorex) simply list "Parity-enabled DRAM".
The www.specbench.org page gives very few specifics
about the Digital test systems.

Please e-mail and post, if possible.



