I see three flaws in your argument.  

(1) DEC is just plain out of their collective minds when it comes to
placing computers in 3 broad classes, and then leaving it that way forever.
I have an "enterprise class" VAX4000-500 used by 10 people, and as you
might imagine, I cannot afford *any* DEC software for this machine because
the enterprise class license costs are insane.  I run Pathworks on this
computer, free software, and software that we've developed in-house.
That's it.  DEC would not be losing *anything* were they to reclassify
these old machines, since nobody will pay the exhorbitant cost of
enterprise-class licenses anyhow.

(2) This discussion, as I understand it, was not about enterprise-
class machines and VMS costs thereon, but rather it was about
scalability down to the desktop and low-end prices of VMS.  So
comparing costs to NTAS is entirely valid for these smaller boxes;
that's what customers, trade-rag pundits, and IS managers do.
So why on Earth would it not be in DEC's best interest to sell
a VMS license at NTAS levels for desktop or even department-class
Alphas?  Are they afraid they'll sell too many computers?  Are
they afraid that Bill Gates will take his ball and go home?

(3) Software support isn't what it used to be, and I've repeatedly
seen upgrades break old software and go unaddressed for months.
I can't imagine how any manger of enterprise-class computers could
survive at V7.0 or 7.1; they'd have hundreds of people screaming
at them and nobody (left) at DEC will fix it.  So, after the hatchet
men have gone through Colorado, we can no longer claim DEC is sinking
big bucks into the support of VMS.  The last guy to try to help me
with a stupid VMSmail problem said he could escalate to engineering,
but "nothing will be done over there because there's nobody left".
He was right.  My VMSmail problems have remained for 4 months.

