From: jleslie@dmccorp.com (Jerry Leslie)
Subject: System Disk Disappears During Conversational Boot
Date: 1997/08/17
Message-ID: <5t7sl5$ssv@igate.dmccorp.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 264842020
Summary: ALPHAStation's System Disk Disappears During Conversational Boot
Organization: Aspen Technology, Inc 713/313-5000
Keywords: alphastation,vms,firmware,conversational,boot
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


We have an AlphaStation 400 4/233 (PALcode 5.48) running OpenVMS V6.2
that cannot be conversationally booted reliably from its boot disk,
dka0; i.e.:

  >>>boot -fl 0,1 dka0

The system issues a message that it cannot open dka0, and then returns 
to the ">>>" prompt. A subsequent "show devices" no longer shows dka0
as being in the configuration, even though a "show devices" prior to 
the conversational boot included dka0 !

There wasn't any problem performing a normal boot, except a mistake
in SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM, which was the reason for the conversational boot 
in the first place.

Here are the SCSI devices on the system, and the firmware revision
level of each device, per SCSI_INFO.EXE:

  DKA0:
    $!      Product Identification: RZ28     (C) DEC
    $!      Product Revision Level: 442D

  DKA100
    $!      Product Identification: RZ26     (C) DEC
    $!      Product Revision Level: 392A

  DKA300:
    $!      Product Identification: RZ29B    (C) DEC
    $!      Product Revision Level: 0016

  DKA400:
    $!      Product Identification: RRD43   (C) DEC
    $!      Product Revision Level: 1084

  MKA500:
    $!      Product Identification: TLZ07     (C)DEC
    $!      Product Revision Level: 553B

The conversational boot was finally accomplished by setting
the AUTO_ACTION to HALT, and power-cycling the system.

Once the SYSTARTUP error was corrected, each disk was checked out
using a program to read every block of a disk, whether it's allocated
or not. No disk errors were found from that program, or 
ANALYZE/DISK_STRUCTURE/REPAIR.

Is there a firmware revision problem with the RZ28 or some other
component that causes the system disk to "disappear" from the system
configuration when a conversational boot is attempted ?

Thanks in advance,

--Jerry,

Gerald (Jerry) R. Leslie  jerry.leslie@aspentech.com  Aspen Technology, Inc.
                          (my opinions are strictly my own)




