Terry Shannon wrote:
> Reducing the cost of enterprise-class OVMS licenses to NTAS levels would
> not be in DEC's best interests.

Lets disregard any volume promises Digital may have made to Microsoft,
promises which forced Digital to steer customers to MS products so that
Digital can acheive those promised volumes.

With such promises disregarded, why would it not be in DEC's interest to
ADD to the revenue base of VMS the sale of low end desktop machines
giving DEC that extra $600 ?

In other words, such added revenus, while minimal to DEC's accountants,
would be icing on the cake to the VMS group who would see some added
revenus, and most importantly, would give credence to DEC's attempt at
reversing the "VMS is DEAD" rumour it itself started a while back.

By making VMS affordable, you will bring additional applications, users
and make it a more interesting platform and this can only help large
customers who will have an easier time convincing their suppliers to
make their apps run on VMS.



