Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


jpg wrote:
> 
> I do not know much about 32/64bit and x86/Alpha RISC instructions,
> so could someone please explain when Intel finaly go 64bit and drop
> the x86 instruction processors (NT is going 64 bit)  and software
> manufactures start producing software for the new Intel chips, will
> these apps work on existing 21164/21264 processors and motherboards
> as if they were ported for Alpha, or will their be a new ver. of
> FX!32 type emulation software, or am I off track ???
> 
>   jp
There have been some patent disclosures of intel patents about Merced
(the 64 bit Intel design) which show there'll be a completely new
instruction set in the things (with some kind of compatibility mode
to execute x86 instructions). You'll need to look on altavista or
some such to find them...
At any rate, experience with other architectures suggests that to get
to the higher speed of the new instruction set, it will be necessary
to change basically everything over to it (else the machine will tend
to spend most of its time running the slower inst. set).
So if NT goes 64 bit on Intel, you can expect a bunch of major
discontinuities. The current "64 bit" stuff in Windows NT is a VERY
few limited APIs designed to let one have static buffers in real
memory addresses in 64 bit address space. No paging, no execution,
not much of anything except the ability to read/write them as
data areas, and enough kludging to get I/O to seem to access them.

I/O to such buffers can be emulated by copying to/from a buffer
in 32 bit space and doing the I/O to the buffer. VMS I/O on the other
hand really does accept 64 bit addresses all the way down the I/O
chain, not needing data copying.

There's a LOT of work needed to make an OS run in 64 bits when
starting with a 32 bit system...high level design docs 1000 pages
long kind of LOT...

Doing it successfully requires a great deal of attention to detail
and organization. I'm not holding my breath waiting for NT to get
there...
Note that some applications (very large databases) can gain a lot
even from the ability to use huge memory buffers in static memory
though. The limited work using Alpha (where the 64 bit space is
basically statically mapped) can therefore produce some real gains
for software willing to exploit the features.

Apart from the patent disclosures on the net, I've heard very little
about Merced, so can't say whether it might have a static memory
map similar to that used with NT on alpha. (Obviously the maps used
by Digital Unix and VMS are not static, but are managed by their
respective OSs, in case there was any confusion.)



