>solutions ? Any problem like that ?
>
>Thanks for any help that would keep my files safe.
>
>Regards,
>
>R. Mendes
>




NEVER trust in any standard that is as weak as SCSI 
(or even worse, much worse, EIDE, for that matter...)

What you describe sounds to me like some bugs in the drive's 
firmware. A busy Alpha server machine, esp. with VMS drivers
using tagged command queuing, put a MUCH higher i/o load on 
disks than most other systems, esp. PCs and the like.

It is thus very good at uncovering hidden bugs (timing errors etc)
in a drives controller/firmware. So go and have the newest firmware
revision put on this disk.

Of course, I assumed that you DID check all SCSI cabling and 
termination etc and made shure all this is ok. BTW: Sometimes active
terminators *seem* to be a bit better than passiv ones.



If you want us to give you more info, it might be helpful to post a few
relevant messages from the errorlog, which contain much more detailed 
info. (have a look at HELP anal /err   to find out how to do this)


