In article <01bca717$0232c7a0$9e3daccf@JamieCampbell.erols.com>, "Jamie Campbell" <crestarj@erols.com> writes:

:A friend with an older VAX 11/750 running VMS 5.4 asked me for help getting
:his system back up after his system disk had to be replaced.
:
:He has a good backup taken a few mon hs ago in standalone mode, but we need
:a way to boot the system.

  The VAX-11/750 has sockets for four boot ROMs, labeled "A" thru "D"
  on the front panel selector switch.  "A" is usually the DDA0: device,
  the console TU58 drive.  "D" is usually the local system's primary
  disk drive.

  If you were able to make a standalone BACKUP several months back, then
  use the same procedure that was used to boot standalone -- I am here
  assuming that the TU58-based standalone BACKUP kit was used -- and
  simply reverse the BACKUP command parameters on the command previously
  used to create the BACKUP saveset now being restored.

  If one really needs BOOT58, one needs more than just BOOT58, one needs
  the full console cassette.  (BOOT58 is located on the VAX-11/750 console
  TU58 cassette.)  And -- if one booted standalone BACKUP off a now-corrupt
  or now no-longer-available disk, one also now needs the OpenVMS VAX
  standalone BACKUP kit on TU58 media.

  The conversational (TU58) bootstrap requires:

    - key switch to LOCAL
    - power-on-action to HALT.
    - load the console media (TU58) in the DDA0: drive
    - the following console commands:
      <CTRL/P>
      >>> H
      >>> I
      >>> U
      >>> B/800 DDA0
      BOOT58>

  You'll then want to @DEFBOO.CMD or similar, to see what commands
  are used at BOOT58> prompt.  You'll then likely end up halting
  the bootstrap process, and rebooting to BOOT58>, and manually
  entering each BOOT58> command, with appropriate updates, for
  whatever device you are bootstrapping.

  If booting standalone BACKUP from TU58, use the above sequence
  and then -- the usual BOOT58> TU58 command -- is:

      BOOT58> @CSABOO.CMD

  The bootstrap will ask you to swap TU58 cassettes as needed.
  Make sure you wait until the system is no longer accessing the
  TU58 before swapping it.

  One uses @SYS$UPDATE:CONSCOPY to make console TU58 backups, and one
  uses @SYS$UPDATE:STABACKIT to create standalone BACKUP kits.

  Copies of the console TU58 cassette, as well as TU58-based standalone
  BACKUP kits, are available from the folks at 1-800-DIGITAL.

:If you have this program on a TU58 cartridge, please call me at (804)
:270-8237. or e-mail me.

  Ask here, get an answer here.

