OK, let's see what I can remember about the Nautilus family (I'll include
PolarStar here too).

8530 - Crippled microcoded version of the 8800 series CPU.
8550 - Normal 8800 CPU in a single CPU cabinet - no multiprocessing expansion.
8700 - Dual processor 8800 with only one CPU installed. Can be upgraded
       (at vast expense) to an 8800. We did this once and the machine
       was down for over a week until we got the second CPU installed.
8800 - 8700 with second CPU installed :-)
88x0 - Polarstar - just like an 8800 only different :-)
       had from 2 to 4 CPUs which were very similar to the 8800 CPU with
       one module different. Internal arrangement of modules different too.
       With all PRO380s gone to act as 8800 consoles, these systems had
       a uVAX-II console running uVMS 4.5.

Common problems:

1) The backplane
2) See 1) :-)
3) The diagnostics which would reliably point to the module that didn't
   need replacement.

However, if your 8xx0 was running it very rarely stopped, just when it did
it was hard to get going again. Our last 8800 was replaced with a 6620 after
Digital FS spent two weeks trying to find out what was wrong. At that time
I'd maintained three 8800s and an 8820 for two years and I couldn't find
out what was wrong either and we swapped just about everything apart from the
backplane (see 1) above.

At one stage we had the largest collection of 8xx0 systems running in Australia
(4 8800s and the 8820) - not something to be proud of though....

Now all I have left of them is a couple of PRO380s and a uVAX-II console.
