Chuan Wang wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> 
> I have the following macro in the c++ code and compiled on a DEC
> Unix machine, it complained about the invalid declaration.
> 
> #define DEFINE_INT_METHODS_BASIC(className) \
>         className& className::operator=(INT16 val) \
>                 {value = val; return *this;}
> 
> the compiler complained at the return value className&.  It looks the DEC
> Unix doesn't take the reference return value.  The macro has been
> successfully compiled on Solaris, AIX and HP-UX.  Anybody can gave me a
> hint why this happened?  Thanks.

Hi.

Macros are considered unwanted resedue of assembler in C and C++.
Templates are supposed to eliminate the need for macros in C++, I.m not
sure if they succeeded in it, though. Anyway, macros are pre-processed,
so if compiler complains, try eliminating the macro as a potential
error.

Try the whole thing without macro. If it does not work, then it's the
problem with C++ syntax. On the other hand, if it works, then there's
something wrong with the macro itself. Try just pro-compiling the macros
only, with cpp command.

Hope this helps.
Nix.



