Erik Toubro Nielsen wrote:
> 
> Can I use Dec Unix on a clone with:
> 
>   PC164 motherboard, Alpha 21164-P5 CPU 366Mhz, 64Mb MCT 60 ns RAM,
> Matrox Mill. PCI card, ZNYX 10/100 Mbit ethernet card, Quantum Stratos
> 1.6Gb AT-BUS Fireball ST, IDE CD Rom drive?
> 
> And what is the SRM and ARC console?
> 
> --
> Erik

Hi Erik,

Of your configuration the motherboard is the only supported component.
Matrox Millenium could perhaps give you a VGA console at the most. I
think the right way to go is to get a SCSI solution. I would prefer
a supported SCSI adapter from us (a narrow KZPAA-AA or an Ultrawide
SN-KZPBA-CA). Digital UNIX 4.0C does support IDE CD-ROMs, but a much
safer option is a SCSI CD-ROM (from Digital it would be 12x PBXRD-DA).

Most SCSI drives should work. For a network interface try to find a
21X40 based NIC. (PCI-based DE500, DE435 Digital codes). If you need
a graphics head, you might squeeze in a S3-based card and it might
work. A nice 2D card would be PowerStorm 3D30 (PBXGB-AA) and for
a 3D solution I would consider something from the 4DT series.

So generally it would take some $$$ to have Digital UNIX run on it.
Your configuration is much more like a Linux system.

ARC is the console Windows NT-based Alpha systems use. ARC is still
used for some Alpha models, but the newer ones have AlphaBIOS which
does the same things ARC does.

SRM is the not so easy to use console which you would need for
Digital UNIX and OpenVMS.

Remember that you should have a Base License for your system. This
is a license you get with the system. If you bought the mbd
for Linux or Windows NT use, you might not have the Digital UNIX
Base license.

Regards,
Pete

