Daniel A. Seagraves <dseagrav@bsdserver.tek-star.net> writes:
> OUT and IN are sequence connectors.  The OUT side has a little terminator
> on it.  The IN side is not terminated.  The drive comes to the READY state
> when I spin it up, no errors.  The head is unlocked.  I say "B DU0" to
> ODT, the processor goes to the RUN state, and nothing happens.  When the
> terminator is removed from the OUT side, the drive refuses to spin up.

  That's the way it's supposed to be - single drives have a sequence termina-
tor, while multi-drive cabinets have one and sequence cables between them. 
This is to keep the rack from tripping circuit breakers after a power failure.

  Staying in RUN and not doing anything indicates the bootstrap is retrying
because it doesn't see a drive. Some common problems are:

  1) not having an *ODD* number of SDI cables and an *EVEN* number of SDI
     bulkheads (the normal config is drive w/ cable to bulkhead to BC26V
     cable to bulkhead to controller cable).
  2) not having the A or B port buttons in (push 'em both in - that's harm-
     less).
  3) having a bad cable or bent pin.
  4) having a bad or incompletely seated unit ID plug.

  The drive's A or B lights should come on during the boot phase. Also, the
controller (UDA50 in your case) should start displaying a "landing lights"
pattern (the controller LED's all strobing in sequence). If you get some-
thing else on the controller LED's, you have a hardware problem - post what
lights are on/flashing and I'll tell you what the manual says.

