steve wrote:
> 
>  Is it possible to setup up POP mailer on Digital Unix 3.2c with enhanced
> security activated?  If so could you point in the direction where I could
> find info regarding setup.
> 
> Thanks for any help.
> 
> Steve Brown
> sbrown@bethel.cs.laurentian.ca

<<< USELESS POP WITH DU >>>

Digital UNIX - OSF/1 come with RAND Corp. Mail Handler, which contains
POPd. It has its own User/Password database which has no connection to
the User/Password of Digital UNIX (yes, it works with enhanced security
:->). Also, you must setup mail delivery, there is a program for
transfering the mail from system default mail box to POP mail drop. It
conforms to POP3 specification (I think), but...

But it lacks one of the most commonly used optional commands in POP3
specification. The missing command is UIDL, Unique message ID List, used
in situations with 2 and more messages in one mail drop. This command is
issued by all comercial e-mail clients, like IE3.x, NN3.x and Pegasus
Mail, when they are configured to delete mail from server upon receipt.

Also, there was another interesting problem with it. The clients were
not reading the mail! Even when there was only one message, client would
issue all necessary commands and the text of the mail message would
travel to the client, but no client would SHOW it??!! The only thing I
could think of was CR/LF conversion (POP specification defines CR/LF as
NewLine, UNIX uses only LF), so I gave it up.

<<< QPOP >>>

Then I downloaded qpop V2.4b1. It works the oposite of POPd, using
system User/Password-s and accessing system Mail Box
(/var/spool/mail/<USER>) directly. As you can see it is still an
unfinished version. I had some small trouble with it, but managed to
resolve it quickly. Actually, it just needed a final touch. If you don't
want to mess with changing original config files get V2.3, it should
work just out of the box.

If you're still interested in V2.4b1, then her are instructions for
setting it up properly.

1. Run ./configure. It will set up the Makefile.

2. Edit Makefile. Add HAVE_SET_AUTH_PARAM define to CFLAGS. It must be
set if you're running enhanced security. I have reported this to the
QPOP maintaner at QualComp and they said they'll include the fix ASAP.

3. Add all additional features to CFLAGS variable, like APOP, POPUID,
AUTHFILE and NONAUTHFILE.

4. Read The Fine Manual for anything else you'd like configure.

5. make

That should be all.
You can download QPOP archives from ftp.qualcomp.com (I forgot the exact
path).

*** A notice to those using Digital UNIX 4.0x.

Digital has changed system calls for protected password accessing. The
calls now have prefix "es" in front of "pwd" instead of "pr"
(getespwnam( ... ) instead of getprpwnam( ... )). Old calls will work,
because they are redirected to the new ones. Still, it is recomended to
switch to new ones, so I did it and sent it to QualComp. Hope they've
included it all.

Good luck with your POP,
Nix.



