Yes, but double-sided surface mount boards are soldered in a high-temp vapor
bath.

>  Perhaps the daughterboards are just DRAM chips (and maybe drive resistors)
>and you could use them as sort-of-SIMMs?

I've got a 16Mb NMI memory board (from an 8550 we trashed *years* ago).  The
daughter cards are DRAM (256Kx1) and LS244(5?) buffers.  There's not much
to the daughter cards.  I thought of re-using these boards myself, but
at this point, why would I want something nearly the size of a pack of
playing cards with a whopping 2Mb when I can use a 72-pin SIMM with an
industry standard interface.  I don't have enough DEC memory boards to justify
the engineering time.

>> Thanks for the info, I don't have the money to get the specs off of Digital, I 
>> was hoping to find someone (out of the 500 sold) who HAD a VAX 8800 and scrapped
>> it, and still had the docs lying around, or some such thing....

By the time the 8800 was shipping, DEC stopped giving out the maintenance
print sets.  I've got prints for the 750, but I think the MicroVAX was the
herald of the age of the disposable VAX.

>  Weren't the 85x0, 8700, 8800, and 897x all the same sort of box running at
>different speeds and with different multiprocessor expandability options? That
>would increase your chances...

Yup.  They were all NMI-based boxes with CPUs and memory sitting on the
Nautilus bus, with peripherals attached via NMI-BI adapter.  Horrid, slow,
power-sucking monsters.  We only got one because our product had a bug on
NMI machines; worked fine on 82x0/83x0 and 6xxx machines.  It turns out
that the NMI-BI adapters works substantially different from the 82x0 CPU
and the XMI-BI adapter, if you try to STOP a BI device (the fix: change the
driver to halt and reboot our board but never issue a BI STOP transaction).

-ethan
