Hello guys/gals I have a bit of bother with my Alphas...


What I'm doing is swapping some nodes around to free up the more
powerful ones for a new project.

What I did today was to swap a 2100 4/233 for a 2000 4/233. The 2100
has 512MB of RAM and the 2000 256 MB. Both have dual EISA DSSI
adapters for connecting the disks (via HSD10's). The 2100 also had an
EISA FDDI card. The 2000 was itself given a FDDI card from another
2100 identical to the one being replaced (that I had spare from
another swap :)  They also both have the usual 10mb Network card. 

I am running VMS 6.2 (with a few ECO's) and DECNET-OSI. As I was
taking the server down to less memory and swapping the network
interface, I updated to a newer version of the firmware (3.8 cd) and
reran the ECU. Everything seemed fine there, at the console level.
Then I booted the 2000 minimum, ran AUTOGEN and NET$CONFIGURE to sort
out the memory and any possible network card address changes (I think
they were on auto anyway) 

However, when I boot fully, the machine seems to get stuck at the
point of starting up DECNET, just after a process creation, which
normally would whizz past. The system disk seems to be accessed
slightly, but this could be a vain attept of the system to carry on.

I suspect a few things, that I will look at tomorrow.

1) The firmware update is not compatible - even though it says it is.

2) When I ran the ECU (Ver 1.8) to set up the DEFEA (FDDI) card, I
used the config file on the ECU disk, not the one that came on disk
with the Card originally.

3)Perhaps the system parameters are not in order - however, the
machine was using a fraction of it's memory before, much less than
256MB.

4) Some interrupt conflict between the DSSI controllers and the FDDI
card (wrong slot number?) 

Does anyone have any ideas/suggestions/etc that may be of help ??

Thanks in advance...

-Rob Stevens-
DERA Farnborough, UK.




