Subject: Re: Need Quiet Cooling for Tadpole ALPHABook
Date: 1997/08/08
Message-ID: <33EAEB06.9E098F0@star.enet.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 262864159
References: <5sak2a$1ar@igate.dmccorp.com>
Organization: OpenVMS Engineering
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.laptops


Jerry Leslie wrote:
> 
> We purchased a Tadpole Technologies ALPHABook to use in giving demos
> of some of our OpenVMS products, at trade shows or prospective customers'
> sites.
> 
> One problem we're having is the system will not keep running at its
> maximum CPU speed without an external cooling source, such as a fan.
> If the system gets too warm, the CPU slows down to protect the system.
> 
> The fan solution is too obtrusive for most trade shows. Is there
> something that can serve as a base for the ALPHABook to lower the
> operating temperature to the point that the unit will run at its
> maximum CPU speed ?
> 
>

  The best way to cool a Tadpole is idle time ;-).  I have an AlphaBOOK,
  and I find that it usually runs within normal temps except when it is
  running something that is compute bound for an extended time.  And
  that does not mean that the application needs to be number
crunching...
  Softwindows for instance is always in a COM state even when completely
  idle.

  Are your demos doing anything that would tend to keep the system in a
  COM state?  Can they be modified to allow some idle time?

  As part of the idle loop on the Tadpole, the CPU speed is dropped,
when
  it leaves the idle loop, it is raised.  The CPU will eventually
overheat
  if it runs at full speed too long, and then the speed is reduced to
let
  it cool, and reduced again if the temerature continues to rise (which
it
  will, if the CPU remains in a COM state).  The lowest speed will allow
the
  CPU to cool even if the system remains in a COM state.

  I've considered adding logic to the graphics driver to slow the CPU
speed
  when the screen saver activates (it would, of course, need to be
optional).

  The case is a heat sink, so I imagine that if you were to make a large
  think aluminum plate with fins on the bottom, and possibly a small DC
  fan to push air through them - it would help to cool the box.  You
need
  to make sure that the plate makes the best possible fit with the
bottom
  to conduct as much heat as possible.

-- 
