Colin,

ET6000???  Try http://www.tseng.com   The ET6000 is a Tseng Labs chip,
and Tseng has generic drivers at their web site for all their chips
and most operating systems.  There is little difference, if any,
between any sort of DEC card with the ET6000 and a generic ET6000
design.  

To DEC's benefit, the "originality" went out of the DEC personal
computer hardware designs several years ago, at least from the point
of view of software.  Now, DEC graphics, SCSI adapters, etc etc etc
use standard run-of-the mill chipsets with equally standard
run-of-the-mill software drivers, even tho their hardware designs
sometimes are proprietary and kinky.  The Celebris, Venturis, and
Prioris all run off-the-shelf Windows 3.x, Windows 95, Windows NT and
whatever else.  But the hardware layout remains unmistakably DEC (or
any other old line computer manufacturer trying to "add value" with a
proprietary hardware solution).  So if your power supply blows out or
the motherboard goes up in smoke, you are forced to pay large bucks
for replacements unless you have an extortive maintenance contract or,
better still, are lucky enough to have the computer still under
warranty.  In this respect, DEC is no different from IBM, Compaq (old
line company mindset), HP, or lots of other leading brands or wannabe
leading brands.

Sorry for the above diatribe, DEC employees and fans, but that's the
way I see it.  Of course, DEC is now lots better than the days of the
Rainbow and the Ken Olsen decrees that personal computers are toys...
Ben Myers

