I can't seem to find any figures on this at all. Can you please
pass your references on to me? Thanks.


>Well, your assesment disagrees with most analysts, presenting a unified theme
>for entire company is generally a good thing, it prevents sometimes confusing or
>contradictory messages.

On the contrary, Digital's message at this point is confused as
hell. What operating system does Digital suggest and support? DU?
VMS? NT? How are these positioned? Is Digital a hardware vendor?
Why do they charge different prices for Alpha workstations depending
on what OS they can support (not supplied, mind you, but can
support)? Why do they produce cheaper Alpha chips that are incapable
of running some of their own operating systems, and then have a
huge artificial price differential (far greater than the manufacturing
cost difference) between the two types of chips? Are we supposed
to be able to migrate from Windows NT to DU? If so, why do they
produce some significantly (and artificially) cheaper machines that
don't allow this migration? If not, why do they produce nearly
identical models where the only difference is that you can migrate?

By having massive cross-subsidisaion across what should be entirely
unrelated prduct lines (workstations and operating systems, for
example), and by letting one part of Digital cripple another part,
they present a confusing front and weaken themselves internally.
Sure, Windows NT competes very well against DU; the Windows NT
folks can go to Digital management and say `go cripple DU's marketing
in such-and-such a way to give us an advantage.' Unfortunately,
they can't do the same for other Unix vendors, and giving them this
sort of artificial support means that they don't develop the strength
to market their product on its own merits.

And, of course, in terms of support having competing groups within
the company makes sense to a consumer. I know that if I'm talking
to the VMS guys, they're going to think that VMS is the best, and
they're going to work hard and without interference to make VMS a
good product. As it stands, I'd be scared to buy an OS (or any
software product) from Digital because even if there is a committed
group within Digital supporting that product, the rug may well be
yanked out from under them due to some sort of `strategic' move,
just like it was for the ALL-IN-1 folks.

> >[re the 64-bit port of NT]
>That was stated to show Microsoft support of Alpha NT, if they were going to
>drop Alpha it's likely they wouldn't bother.

No, it is still quite likely that they would bother. Microsoft is
not short of development resources. They are, however, quite short
of 64-bit development experience. To get this experience, you have
to do a 64-bit port. Microsoft could wait two years for Intel to
produce a stable and solid 64-bit CPU, and start the port from zero
then. Or they could do a port now on the Alpha and two years down
the road start with a huge body of problems fixed in the
machine-independent code and a lot of experience doing the MI code,
cutting a huge amount off their development time. Even if they drop
the Alpha completely, and never do a single release of 64-bit NT
for the Alpha, it would still be well worthwhile to do the port.

