Summary: added bonus: patch to pop3d to work w/ ditto
Organization: NASA Goddard Space Flight Center -- Greenbelt, Maryland USA
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


We turned on password shadowing on a RISC Ultrix 4.4 machine, which
DEC calls "enhanced password security" (see secsetup(8)), because it
seems like a real good idea.  But when you do this secsetup apparently
tweaks some other knob so that su can only be done from a "secure"
terminal.  The bottom line is that you can become root only by
displacing the guy that's using the workstation, logging him off, and
logging in as root.  In other words, you can't really su from anywhere
(except terminals connected to the serial ports, but call us silly, we
don't have terminals connected to our workstations).

From reading the manual, I see only one fix, which is to mark all the
pseudoterminals in /etc/ttys as secure.  However, this has the effect
that anybody anywhere can use telnet to directly log in as root.  This
is a little too unsecure for our taste.

What we would like is to have the original su behavior, which is that
direct root login can only happen at the workstation, but any other
terminal can su.  Anybody know how to do this while keeping the shadow
password?

Any help much appreciated, system maintenance is becoming a real chore
like this.

 - Dave Cottingham

PS  As a special added bonus, I have put together a patch to pop3d to
make it work with "enhanced password security."  Just posted it to
alt.sources.



