In article <01bc9db4$a523eb40$5414a8c0@spreble>, "DeSai Systems, Inc" <spreble@desai.com> writes:
:Can I use the x86 ver of VB? or VC++, etc? (Using, FX!32 that is). Or do I
:have to get an Alpha Version? Does one exist? What other alternatives are
:there for programming under NT on an Alpha? If I can use FX!32 with VB,
:what limitations are there?

  Check the product box and the product documentation and see if the
  particular Microsoft Visual compiler package in question can generate
  Alpha code -- various compiler kits can generate binaries for either
  platform.  If the package generates Alpha code, this would be the
  prefered solution here.

  If the Microsoft compiler package only generates Intel code and no
  mention of Alpha code generation capabilities is made, then you will
  have to try translating the resulting code via FX!32.  (You may also
  have to translate the compiler package itself, if that is permitted
  by your product license.  But realize an Intel compiler package
  translated for use on Alpha will still generate Intel code...)

  For the limitations around what FX!32 can translate, please see the
  FX!32 documentation.

  You might also want to try looking around in and/or asking in one or
  two of the herd of Microsoft-oriented newsgroups around.

