Oh man, what a loaded question. We're the best! We have 64 bit computing
now, and have had for a number of years. We know all the ways to make the
hardware work with 64 bit efficiently, everyone else has yet to learn.
DIGITAL UNIX is 64 bit, nobody else has that.

Lest anyone say that anyone else has 64 bit, with the statement that they
saw it in an ad. well, they may have 64 bit, but not as it is properly
defined. For example, IBM and HP both say they have 64 bit computers, and
they do, sort of, they have a 32 bit computer that acccess memory 64 bits
at a time. So they say 64 bit computing. Now we did this years ago, more
than 10 years ago in fact, but we always told the truth, we had 32 bit VAX
computers with 128 bit memory data buses. So, we could have done like IBM
and HP are doing today, and 10 years ago we could have been claiming 128
bit computers. C'mon, let's be real, this is semantic playing.

The proper and within the engineering community way to define the actual
size of a computer is the standard storage size of the registers that
manipulate the data in them. An Alpha processor is filled with 64 bit
registers, there is not a single 32 bit register amongst them. Thus an
Allpha is 64 bits. An HP PA RISC is filled with 32 bit registers, thus it
is a 32 bit processor. An IBM PowerPC is again a 32 bit processor.

Let's be truthful here. DIGITAL is and will continue to be the leader in
64 bit computing and in semiconductor design. When we introduced the
Alpha, we stated that the architecture was scalable well beyond the year
2000 and much more. In the past 4 years we have traversed from what,
133Mhz to now almost 600Mhz. You can rest assurred that we can and will go
much faster in clock rates.

To the real question, if you are doing number crunching Hugh, why do you
want to use a server to do number crunching? If in fact, you want to do
number crunching, then maybe you want to sit down with one of our
knowledgeable sales staff and actually configure a system that is
optimized for your number crunching. Things that come to mind are, cache
size, I/O throughput (ie; storage, aka Fast Ultra Wide SCSI), memory size,
512MB, 2GB, data bus size, 64 bit, 128 bit, 256 bit, RAID disk storage,
PCI bus versus EISA bus, etc... DO you actualy model any numbers visually?
If so, then you want to consider some of our real high performance OpenGL
hardware accelerated cards.

I'm sorry Hugh, you asked a loaded question and I just relish writing at
length. An AlphaServer 1000A has a nice price tag, but, if it's number
crunching you really want to do, then you'd best stop, take a look at what
you want to do and optimize the hardware to do it. In addition to this, I
assume that the three HP machines are already paid for, so in fact, they
occupy floor space and consume electrical and cooling, but that's about
it. If this is the case, then any plans that you do make should well
include the continued effective use of these now inexpensive resources in
your environment. Coming to my mind, is that there may be some light
number crunching that some users do, or maybe, occasional number
crunching, and if this is the case, those users could be directed towards
the HP machines. For the real heavy hitters, they get directed towards the
Alpha.

Think about it. You have good hardware with you now, and you want to add
more good hardware, your end goal should be to maximize your return on
investment, both current and future.

Regards, Richard Tomkins

