Hi,

I'm researching the evolution of RISC machines and I noticed all the
92/93 papers introducing the Alpha & Alpha architecture are all
footnoted to two papers that describe the PRISM [ISSCC 89, Conrad et. al
"A 50 MIPS (peak) 32/64b Microprocessor" or the RIP (reduced instruction

parallel/pipelined architecture) [Compcon Spring 90, Bhandarkar et. al.

"High Performance Issue Oriented Architecture".  The second paper is far

more interesting and describes the Conrad article chip as the scalar
part of a Cray like supercomputer chip set. Later ACM papers on the
Alpha describe the Conrad chip as the major influence. Looks like this
was the parent or step parent of the Alpha.



Anybody know anything more about the Alpha genealogy? Why did Dec decide

to try a super & then drop it?



Thanks



George Sawyer



