A friendly note from IBM?  Unfortuneately that article seemed a bit suspect at points.
It seemed the author wanted to prove a point and used evidence to support that argument
and ignored counter evidence.  He states Digital has not made money on Intel machines,
they have over $2 billion dollars in revenue a year and have posted a profit in the last three
quarters.  He states they have no significant share of any important market, from what I 
understand Digital has done extremely well in the internet/ISP market, selling high end
servers to Netscape, Amazon.com and PointCast (which they just announced this week),
among others.  He also fails to note that Digital is getting it's market message coordinated
under Bruce Claflin, presenting a unified vision for the entire company.
   As far as Microsoft support for Alpha NT goes, they just released native Alpha versions
of Word and Excel this week and are first porting 64bit NT to Alpha.  Digital also says NT
Alpha revenue has been growing quite well in the last few quarters.  As far as market
place penetration goes, the Alpha has been doing well in markets it should go after, like 3D
animation.  I read a press release (http://www.carrera.com/good_news.html) from Carerra 
(a Alpha machine OEM) that stated Digital Domain has 230 (Carerra) Alpha workstations 
generating 3D effects for the movie Titanic and Babolyn 5, commercials etc.  Desktstation
web page also has a section on effects done with Alpha (www.dti.com, click on testimonials).
I was recently at the PC Expo and asked a couple clone makers how there business is
