Jason Patterson wrote:
> 
> > I'm researching the evolution of RISC machines and I noticed all the
> > 92/93 papers introducing the Alpha & Alpha architecture are all
> > footnoted to two papers that describe the PRISM ...
> 
> I once heard a reasonably strong rumor (started within DEC?) that
> in the name "Alpha AXP", the AXP stood for "Almost eXactly Prism".
> 
> Can anyone from DEC confirm (or deny) this?
> 
> In any case, Dileep Bhandarkar's book "Alpha Implementations and Architecture"
> goes through some brief details of the PRISM project (he was a co-architect)
> and the major differences between PRISM and Alpha.
> 
> Paraphrasing his words, the 3 biggest differences (aside from removing the
> special vector hardware) were:
> 
>   * moving from a unified to a split register file  - the GOOD
> 
>   * removing byte loads & stores  - the BAD
> 
>   * delaying delivery by 3 years  - the UGLY!

Thanks, I'll check out D. Bhandarkar's book this week.  It is really
interesting to look at the PRISM with a separate vector processor for a
full set of vector operations, and the multiTITAN with FP Vector
operations and then see the Alpha with parallel byte operations (not
really vector operations, but ...). Thats a crooked road. Where did they
/ that come from?

I haven't had a chance to digest all the TITAN papers yet, so I'm really
curious to read them *and* see Bhandarkar's book.  I'm quite pleased to
see the TITAN folks looked at VLIW architectures, although obviously
they didn't choose them.  I just read about the ELI project at Yale, and
the MIT Bulldog compiler - cool stuff if you like odd architectures
(which I do. STARAN!) But I digress.

George Sawyer



