re: Digital's pricing of VMS , UNIX and NT.


Here is my take:

In the Olsen days, Digital strived to make VMS truly scalable, from
the desktop to the supercomputer centre. The come the "IBM" style of
pricing and arogance and DEC starts to loose marketshare in the lower
and middle markets where UNIX was much cheaper.

Then comes Palmer who decides that VMS is dead already and  DEC's
marketing proceeds to convince many of Palmer's beliefs.

Palmer then realises that he was killing VMS prematurely and that
revenus were drying up badly because so many people were moving away
from VMS. Digital then decides to stop killing VMS alltogether, and
assesses that the last remaining possible niche is the high end 7-24
