> I once heard a reasonably strong rumor (started within DEC?) that
> in the name "Alpha AXP", the AXP stood for "Almost eXactly Prism".
> 
> Can anyone from DEC confirm (or deny) this?

I'm not from DEC (although I was once their worst nightmare), but I can
assure you that "AXP" meant Almost eXactly Prism only in jest. Matter of
fact, I had that bit of information in my newsletter three years ago.

Actually, AXP stands for nothing. It was just an available (e.g.
non-trademarked) series of three letters. Prior to AXP, DEC was looking
at using Advanced RISC Architecture (ARA)... in fact, some of the very
early Alpha collateral talked about advanced risc architecture. Turns
out that ARA stands for something obscene in a Middle Eastern language.

> 
> In any case, Dileep Bhandarkar's book "Alpha Implementations and Architecture"
> goes through some brief details of the PRISM project (he was a co-architect)
> and the major differences between PRISM and Alpha.
> 

Dileep's book is SUPERB. If you're into this sort of thing, buy a copy.
Numerous Secret Facts are revealed. In fact, several of the first
chapters look, well, very familiar to those who have gazed at the
highly-confidential Prism System Reference Manual.

