jf mezei wrote:
> 
> re: Digital's pricing of VMS , UNIX and NT.
> 
> Here is my take:
> 
> Palmer then realises that he was killing VMS prematurely and that
> revenus were drying up badly because so many people were moving away
> from VMS. Digital then decides to stop killing VMS alltogether, and
> assesses that the last remaining possible niche is the high end 7-24
> market where VMS still has no rival due to its clustering.
> 
> The end result is that VMS is no longer designed to be scalable. Its
> pricing is adjusted to appeal to the big high-end shops as Digital still
> thinks there is no way VMS can compete against NT and UNIX in the low
> and middle markets.

That's a reasonable scenario. There's no way that VMS can compete with
WNT in the low end--even Unix is having its share of difficulties in
this regard. WNT is architecture-independent via the Win32 API, and has
tons of low-cost apps. Such is not the case for VMS.

So yes, VMS is now aimed at the high end. I expect that it will be
pretty damned scalable when it comes to scaling UP, especially when
Galaxy-based systems come out sometime next year.

